WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.639
Film Courage: What part of the screenwriting
process would you say you’re the best at

00:00:03.639 --> 00:00:09.400
and then maybe not so great? Are you great
at the first draft? Not so great with rewrites

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.749
or maybe it’s the opposite?

00:00:11.749 --> 00:00:18.080
Todd Berger, screenwriter: I would say I’m
really good at the first draft let’s say.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:23.310
I’m really good at plotting. Like I’ve
figured out the mathematical formula to a

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:30.199
plot of a movie and being able to figure out
exactly when everything should happen. Because

00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:33.680
really when you think about writing a movie
especially Hollywood movies like I know there

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:38.320
are movie that break the formula but it is
all math, it’s all mathematical formulate

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:42.140
and you need certain things to happen on certain
pages and the inciting incident needs to come

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:47.039
around here. You know the emotional character
journeys to kind of happen on here and you

00:00:47.039 --> 00:00:48.719
need this, that and the other thing.

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:55.609
So I feel like I’m really good at figuring
out a plot and then figuring how it all goes

00:00:55.609 --> 00:01:00.640
down. My weak spot definitely on the first
draft is “Why aren’t any of these characters

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.540
doing this?” Or “Why do we care about
them?” You know, what are the emotional

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:13.360
journeys of the characters sometimes I think
of almost secondary. Like I’m such a plot

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:19.250
person that I love to figure out like what
actually happens in this movie from A. B to

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:22.240
C? How does it start, what is the middle,
what is the end?

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.881
But then I have writer friends who are totally
character people, they think of the character

00:01:26.881 --> 00:01:32.330
first and the entire backstory of that character
and what happens with that character’s emotional

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:37.619
journey. Then they figure out the plot of
the movie. I’ the opposite. I think of the

00:01:37.619 --> 00:01:41.810
plot of the movie and then I figure out who
are these characters really. What do they

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:45.939
want? What are their emotional journeys? And
that’s what I figure out in the rewrites,

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:47.479
like that’s when I go back in.

00:01:47.479 --> 00:01:54.240
But I’m super OCD and I won’t finish a
script…if I start a script, I have to finish

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:03.110
it, I won’t do anything else. And I’ll
write an outline number 1 to 100 (I’ll just

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:07.689
number a page 1 to 100) and I’ll be like
what happens, this is every minute of the

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:10.650
movie, this is every page, what happens on
every page? What happens in every minute of

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:15.660
this movie? One page 1…this is what happens.
Page 15…this is what happens. Page 82…this

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.140
is what happens.&nbsp; Page 100…this is is the
last scene. And I’ll fill in all those gaps

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.880
and I’ll put it on my desk, I’ll print
it up (actually print it out), I’ll put

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:30.910
it on my desk and I’ll actually start writing.
And I’ll be like I’m not going to be happy

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:38.091
until I’ve gotten through all 100 pages
and I’ll just spend…I’ll ignore all

00:02:38.091 --> 00:02:42.850
other aspects of my life. I won’t go out,
I won’t do anything, I won’t go see movies.

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:47.320
I’ll work on it. I’ll wake up at&nbsp;6:30&nbsp;in
the morning and work on it until my brain

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:52.120
is fried and I can’t work on it anymore
and I’ll just do that until I have the first

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:57.230
draft. And the first draft often sucks but
at least it’s done. And then you can set

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:02.150
it aside, take some time off from it and then
come back to it. And then the rewrite is the

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:04.030
fun part for me.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:09.780
Film Courage: Do you always know what’s
going to happen on line 100? Is the end of

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:10.972
it always clear to you?

00:03:10.972 --> 00:03:16.981
Todd: Yes, the first thing I do is I write
down what happens on page 1, what happens

00:03:16.981 --> 00:03:21.480
on page 30. That’s the end of the first
act, that’s your inciting incident, that

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.210
is your movie right? It’s what happened
because what happens on page 30. If you were

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:33.180
going to go see a trailer for the movie page
30 is the thing, it’s in the trailer.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.930
And then I write down what happens on page
100. How does it end? What is the ending?

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:42.040
I need to know where this is going and then
I find out how do we get there and then you

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:44.010
fill in all the holes.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:49.840
On page 80-ish is the end of the second act,
dark of night, what’s gone really bad for

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.560
everybody? Why is this the end of the second
act. And then you fill in all the holes in

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:54.560
between.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:04:05.620
I like to come up with really cool endings,
really cool third acts, and then move backwards

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:09.480
in a way. I want to know where this is all
going and how it ends. I feel like a lot of

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:19.040
people (a lot of writers) spend a lot of time
on their first act and when you see a lot

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.760
of movies the first act is really awesome
and then they just kind of don’t know what

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.880
to do with it. Like they have a really good
idea that they don’t know what to do with.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:32.400
Why I strive really hard to think about the
second half of the movie…to a fault. Often

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:37.840
people’s criticisms of my work is “Yeah,
your first act is really meandering or really

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:39.930
long. But your second half is great.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:44.530
So I try to focus on the second half of the
movie. But the hard part of being a writer

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:47.950
is most people don’t even get to the second
half of the movie when they’re reading your

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:53.720
script. They read the first 10 pages and your
first 10 pages better blow them away.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:58.850
Whereas I often believe in the slow burn of
a first act, take it really slow, let stuff

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:06.400
develop which is to my detriment often. But
I’m getting better, I’m getting better

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:06.410
at it.

